{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## FIXED — Gerrit 1343. Three of the four ACs are met; the live one waits on a harness run.\n\n`_peer_removed_the_parent()` gates the outbound parent SET in `diff_canonical_fields`: suppress only when the local parent is set, the remote has none, AND the observed peer parent still EQUALS the local one. `get_peer_parent` stays getattr-guarded, so a legacy store falls through to the previous unconditional push.\n\n**Gates:** 2170 reconciler tests pass, ruff clean, mypy 429, hygiene 0, `outbound_field_diff.py` 458/800.\n\n### THE IMPLEMENTER REPORTED A CONTRADICTION AND WAS RIGHT TO — I had predicted the wrong casualty\n\nI warned it that guard 4 (`test_same_pass_outbound_parent_write_suppresses_the_clear`) would likely go red and told it not to touch it. **Guard 4 passed untouched.** It hand-builds `_FakeOutbound`, so it never calls the outbound differ and the fix cannot reach it.\n\nWhat went red instead was the **diagnostic cell in the new module** — the one I said must stay green. And that is logically forced, not a regression: that cell CHARACTERISED link 1 of the defect (\"outbound DOES emit parent here\"), which is precisely what the fix removes. A defect-characterisation test cannot survive its own defect being fixed; the two are contradictory over the same fixture by construction. The agent identified this correctly, left both cells untouched as instructed, and handed the decision back.\n\n### What I did with it\n\n**Inverted the diagnostic cell into a regression guard**: outbound must emit NO parent write when the peer removed it. That is a SHARPER guard than the primary cell — the primary asserts the outcome (the local parent ends up cleared), this asserts the mechanism (the contradicting write is never produced). Restoring the re-push reddens it immediately, whereas the primary could in principle be rescued by some unrelated change to inbound's filter.\n\n**Added the opposite-direction cell**, which is the one that stops this fix becoming a worse bug. Suppressing on an empty remote parent ALONE would green everything else here and silently discard real local intent — 'the peer wins a removal it made' quietly becoming 'the peer always wins', which is data loss. `test_outbound_STILL_pushes_a_parent_the_local_side_re_set` holds the scenario identical except the OBSERVED PEER PARENT, and requires the push. It passes, so the suppression is discriminating on evidence rather than on a falsy remote.\n\n**Narrowed guard 4's docstring** rather than its assertion. Its scenario is now specifically the local-re-set case — the one where suppression IS correct — and it records that it fabricates its input, which is exactly why it could never have caught this.\n\n- [x] `test_inbound_clear_survives_a_real_bidirectional_pass` GREEN\n- [x] a genuinely re-set local parent still wins outbound\n- [ ] `test_inbound_clear_parent_round_trips` GREEN on a live run — needs the next harness run\n- [x] guard 4 reworded to the local-re-set case"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785811867075645001,"uuid":"3e89b74a-3635-43a0-b87b-0f3a52bbb469"}